<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuri Kimimasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1829–1909</span> · Also known as Mitsuoka Hachirō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuri Kimimasa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> domain and a financial official of the early Meiji government. A follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yokoi-shonan">Yokoi Shōnan</a>, he reformed Fukui's finances, and in 1868 he wrote the first draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a>, which set out the principles of the new government. He also oversaw the issue of the government's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> and later served as governor of Tokyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuri-kimimasa.jpg" alt="Yuri Kimimasa" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimimasa_Yuri_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
